Track 2: Chalk Skips Over the Unburied Slipper
- Story & Context: This confession piece centers on the aerialist bride Ilona, who spent the morning of the ceremony tracing a children's hopscotch court across the salt floor to calm her tremors, deliberately drawing the final square directly over an unexploded circus mortar shell. The track relies on a solitary, suffocating pipe organ cadence that periodically erupts into frenzied double-time Balkan brass whenever her guilt spikes. Her whispered soprano turns into a full-throated wail as she confesses to swapping the wedding wine for arsenic: "You play by my chalk rules now, my darling, so hop on one broken heel and do not look at the ditch."
- Visual & Lyrics Prompt: Atmospheric top-down view of an intricate hopscotch grid drawn in coarse white chalk across a cracked, black stone floor, with a single antique satin ballet slipper resting on the final square beside a rusted calliope pipe, lit by a singular stark overhead spotlight.

Track 3: Mrtvački Čoček
- Story & Context: Decades after the wedding fire killed both family patriarchs, local villagers claim the deserted salt mine still produces rattling 11/8 rhythm bursts every autumn when the cold wind drafts whistle through the abandoned ventilation shafts. Bandleader Vasile assembled this track by dragging a vintage organ into the flooded cavern to record the spectral resonance against the rock walls, matching his accordion runs to the rhythm of dripping mineral water. The song culminates in a dizzying wall of phantom trumpets and thundering tapan hits as the choir bellows the legendary cautionary myth: "Dance until your kneecaps turn to ash, for no groom leaves the sawdust alive!"
- Visual & Lyrics Prompt: Eerie, grandiose composition of an abandoned underground cathedral carved of pale salt rock, where ghostly, translucent figures of circus performers dance in a manic circle around an ancient, weed-choked pipe organ half-submerged in black reflective water.

